   "source": [
    "# Country bake-off — where does XGBoost actually win?\n",
    "\n",
    "The assignment's recommended countries are four different **data-generating processes**. This notebook checks those labels on real weekly conflict series and then runs the same walk-forward protocol as `src/pipeline.py` (naive / rolling-mean / ETS / XGBoost) to answer:\n",
    "\n",
    "> **On which of these countries would a complex model (XGBoost) forecast better than a simple baseline?**\n",
    "\n",
    "| Code | Country | Assignment claim | What that implies for ML |\n",
    "|---|---|---|---|\n",
    "| `IS` | Israel | regular escalation cycles | trees can use calendar, `weeks_since_spike`, mean-reversion between wars |\n",
    "| `BM` | Myanmar | internal conflict | slow-moving civil war; high persistence → last-value is hard to beat |\n",
    "| `YM` | Yemen | stably high background | rolling mean / ETS should already be excellent; little leftover structure |\n",
    "| `SU` | Sudan | structural breaks | trees trained on the old regime fail; persistence tracks the new level |\n",
    "\n",
    "**Target (Variant A):** weekly event count, horizons \\(h \\in \\{1,4\\}\\) in CV and \\(h \\in \\{1,2,3,4\\}\\) on the 12-week hold-out. Direct multi-horizon, expanding-window walk-forward.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Why UCDP, not GDELT.** The GDELT bulk cache in this repo is Ukraine-only (`ActionGeo_CountryCode = UP`). Re-downloading ~4.7 years of daily GDELT for four extra countries is ~10 GB. UCDP GED 26.1 + Candidate 26.0.6 is the gold-standard lethal-violence event dataset, already geo-coded to these four countries, and is a cleaner measure of *conflict activity* than news-derived CAMEO 18/19 (no media-volume bias). The DGP question — cycles vs persistence vs stable mean vs breaks — is about the conflict process itself; GDELT would add noise on top of the same process. Wikipedia pageviews are added as a Google-Trends analogue (attention leading indicator).\n",
    "\n",
    "Sources: UCDP GED Global 26.1 (Davies, Pettersson, Öberg 2026; Sundberg & Melander 2013) and UCDP Candidate 26.0.6. Cached under `data/raw/ucdp/`.\n"
   ]
  },
