   "source": [
    "### How to read the result\n",
    "\n",
    "Walk-forward CV (2014–2026, mean of \(h=1,4\)):\n",
    "\n",
    "| Country | Naive MASE | Best simple | XGBoost | XGB vs naive | XGB vs best simple |\n",
    "|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n",
    "| Sudan `SU` | 0.98 | roll-mean **0.86** | 0.90 | **+8%** | −5% |\n",
    "| Yemen `YM` | 1.08 | roll-mean **0.95** | 1.01 | **+6%** | −7% |\n",
    "| Myanmar `BM` | 1.22 | roll-mean **1.15** | 1.30 | −7% | −14% |\n",
    "| Israel `IS` | **1.21** | naive 1.21 | 1.67 | **−38%** | −38% |\n",
    "\n",
    "**Headline:** XGBoost never beats the best *simple* model. The leftover after AR(1) on Yemen/Sudan is mostly **local-mean variation**, which a 4-week rolling mean already captures without overfitting. Trees only look better when the floor is last-value.\n",
    "\n",
    "| Country | DGP (confirmed) | Where XGBoost stands |\n",
    "|---|---|---|\n",
    "| **Yemen (`YM`)** | Stable high background, 4% zeros, lowest CV, AR(1)≈0.64, highest STL seasonality. | Cleanest *fair* ML setting: dense, mean-reverting. XGB beats naive, loses to rolling mean. After the 2022 truce, XGB actually **beats naive by +19%** (level drops; persistence stays high). |\n",
    "| **Sudan (`SU`)** | Structural break (Apr 2023, ~6× mean). Moderate AR(1)≈0.67. | Expanding-window CV lets trees eventually see the new regime, so XGB beats naive on average. Train-*before* / test-*after* the war: **XGB −32% vs naive**. Rolling mean still wins the full CV. |\n",
    "| **Myanmar (`BM`)** | Internal war, AR(1)≈0.91, non-stationary (ADF p=0.62). | Ukraine analogue: last-value is almost sufficient. XGB adds noise. Coup split: **XGB −118%**. |\n",
    "| **Israel (`IS`)** | Pulse process (56% zeros) + irregular wars, not a 52-week season (seasonal-naive MASE 2.8). 7 Oct is a surprise (wiki correlates with \(y_{t+1}\) only via the count itself; partial ≈ 0). | **Worst XGBoost country.** Persistence during war; trees smear pre-war quiet into the new plateau. Post-7-Oct split: **XGB −227%**. |\n",
    "\n",
    "If the assignment question is “pick the country where a complex model has the best chance”:\n",
    "\n",
    "1. **Yemen** — only series that is stable enough for lag/rolling features to be the right tool, and the only break-test where XGB helps.\n",
    "2. Sudan — XGB beats naive in mixed-regime CV, but that is smoothing, not break-forecasting; rolling mean is simpler and better.\n",
    "3. Myanmar / Israel — do not pick these to showcase XGBoost. Israel is the brief’s own example of “ML worse than naive — and that’s normal.”\n",
    "\n",
    "Outputs: `outputs/country_bakeoff/` (metrics + figures).\n"
   ]
